{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, I'm Dr. Lee. I’ll be taking care of you during your stay. You came in with fever, nausea, and vomiting—how are you feeling now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A little better. The nausea has calmed down since I got some medicine in the ER. But I was never having belly pain—someone must’ve written that down wrong."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, thanks for clarifying. We saw your outside CT showed some gallbladder wall thickening, so we wanted to check if that was the cause. But your ultrasound today shows no signs of acute cholecystitis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So it’s not my gallbladder? I was worried I might need surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "No, surgery isn’t needed. The ultrasound shows your gallbladder is distended but compressible, and there’s no wall edema. It doesn’t look like active infection."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good to hear. I’ve had enough surgeries already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:21:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We reviewed your history—you’re on warfarin for your mechanical mitral valve, and your INR came back at 6.6 today. That’s higher than we want."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:22:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh, that’s not good. My INR has been all over the place before. Should I stop my warfarin?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:23:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll hold it for now. You’ll get a small dose of vitamin K to help bring it down safely. We’ll restart it once we’re sure it’s under control."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:24:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I trust you. I just don’t want to have a clot or bleed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:25:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. We’re balancing that. You’re also on dialysis—your creatinine is high, but that’s expected. We’ll schedule dialysis tomorrow to help clear some toxins."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:26:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, I’m usually on MWF. I’ve missed one already."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:27:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ve scheduled you for tomorrow at noon. That should help with your overall feeling of being unwell."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2194-08-19 20:28:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. My transplant team always says dialysis makes a big difference."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2194-08-20 08:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. Your labs from this morning show your INR dropped to 10 after the vitamin K. Still high, but moving in the right direction."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2194-08-20 08:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "It’s still that high? I thought the vitamin K would work faster."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2194-08-20 08:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It’s improving. We’ll keep monitoring. Your liver enzymes were a bit elevated yesterday—AST and ALT—but they’re already coming down. That suggests a mild viral illness."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2194-08-20 08:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So this was just a virus? That makes sense—I had a low-grade fever at home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2194-08-20 08:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and your blood cultures show no growth, so we don’t think it’s bacterial. We stopped the antibiotics from the outside hospital."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2194-08-20 08:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’m glad. I don’t need more meds than necessary."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "How did dialysis go?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Smooth. I already feel a bit more clear-headed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. Your repeat labs show your kidney function improved—creatinine down to 4.5. Bicarbonate is normalizing too."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And my INR?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Down to 2.8 this morning—much safer. We’re going to restart your warfarin at 5 mg tonight."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Same dose as before? I was on 2.5 mg last time."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, we’re going back to 5 mg. Your transplant team was consulted—they agree. You have a history of labile INRs, so they want you on the higher dose with close monitoring."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2194-08-20 13:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I remember last time I was on 2.5 mg, my INR dropped too low. Okay, I’ll stick with 5 mg."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2194-08-21 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news—your INR is stable at 2.8, and your liver tests are now normal. You’ve been fever-free for over 24 hours."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2194-08-21 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So I can go home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2194-08-21 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, likely tomorrow morning. We’ll make sure your warfarin plan is clear. You’ll need to check your INR at the lab on Monday."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2194-08-21 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "And if it’s too low?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2194-08-21 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You have Lovenox at home. If your INR is below 2.5, start it as a bridge and call the anticoagulation clinic. They’ll guide you."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2194-08-21 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I’ve done that before. I’ll call them right away."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "All set. Your final labs look good—INR 2.9, kidney function stable, no signs of infection. You’re ready to go home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Great. What about my other meds?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue all your home medications—tacrolimus, prednisone, sevelamer, aspirin. We added tramadol for your hand pain if the lidocaine patches aren’t enough."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you. And I’ll keep weighing myself daily, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, and call if you gain more than three pounds in a day. Follow up with your PCP and transplant team as scheduled."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. Thanks for taking such good care of me."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. Take care, and we’ll see you in clinic if needed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "One more thing—my foot ulcer. Should I get it checked?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, keep it clean and dry. See your wound care team soon. Let us know if it gets red or starts draining."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. I’ve been avoiding it because it hurts to take the bandage off."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Understood. But it’s important to monitor. Maybe ask your home nurse to help."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Good idea. I’ll call them when I get home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. All right, the discharge papers are ready. You can head out whenever you’re packed."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you again, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Take care. Stay well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2194-08-22 08:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2194-08-19 19:56:00",
    "admission_end": "2194-08-22 14:30:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "A woman with end-stage renal disease, a functioning pancreatic transplant, mechanical mitral valve on anticoagulation, and systolic heart failure was admitted for fever, nausea, and vomiting. She was transferred from an outside hospital where she received antibiotics and imaging showed gallbladder wall thickening and stranding around a failed renal graft. Evaluation revealed no acute cholecystitis on ultrasound and no source of bacterial infection; blood cultures were negative. Her symptoms resolved, and liver enzymes normalized, consistent with a viral syndrome. Her INR was supratherapeutic at 6.6, peaking at 10, prompting administration of vitamin K. INR stabilized at 2.9, and warfarin was restarted at 5 mg daily. She underwent one session of hemodialysis, which improved her renal function and overall stability. She was discharged home with instructions to monitor INR, resume her regular dialysis schedule, and follow up with her primary care and transplant teams.",
    "problems": [
      "Supratherapeutic INR",
      "Acute upper respiratory infections of unspecified site",
      "End stage renal disease",
      "Chronic systolic heart failure",
      "Complications of transplanted kidney",
      "Thrombocytopenia",
      "Pancreas replaced by transplant",
      "Diabetes mellitus without mention of complication, type I [juvenile type], not stated as uncontrolled",
      "Ulcer of other part of foot",
      "Other chronic pain",
      "Long-term (current) use of anticoagulants",
      "Renal dialysis status"
    ]
  }
}
